Aerie-Purpose: Hide an extension's cookie allow or session-only rule that could apply to a third-party context while third-party cookies are blocked, so a rule set while they were allowed stops winning over the block, and brings it back if the user allows them again.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: extensions/browser/api/content_settings/content_settings_custom_extension_provider.cc and .h and BUILD.gn; chrome/browser/content_settings/host_content_settings_map_factory.cc
Aerie-Kind: hook

Issue #166, found by a Claude Security scan. Patch 0016 refuses a broad cookie ALLOW or SESSION_ONLY
exception from chrome.contentSettings.cookies.set only while third-party cookies are blocked at that moment.
A rule that an extension set while the user allowed third-party cookies stayed in the store, and an explicit
allow wins over the third-party block (kAllowByExplicitSetting), so it let third-party cookies through again
after the user switched back to blocking.

This patch filters on read, so nothing is dropped. CustomExtensionProvider (the content settings provider for
extension rules) now follows profile.cookie_controls_mode: it keeps the mode in an atomic (rules are read on
other threads than the prefs), updated by a pref observer, and tells its observers when the mode changes.
While third-party cookies are blocked (mode "block", or any incognito session, as in CookieSettings), the
rule iterator and GetRule leave out a cookie ALLOW or SESSION_ONLY rule that is not first-party only (the
primary pattern equals the secondary pattern and names one host, no domain wildcard; the same test as patch
0016, now in CustomExtensionProvider::IsCookieAllowForThirdPartyContext). A first-party allow, every blocking
rule and every rule of another type are read as before, and rules of other providers (the user's) are never
touched. The rule stays in the extension store: if the user allows third-party cookies again it takes effect
again. The factory passes the profile's prefs; the new constructor argument defaults to null, which filters
nothing, so unit tests that build the provider keep working.

